  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe approach the problem by developing a multi-resolution optimization framework that combines global exploration via covariance matrix adaptation evolution strategy (CMA-ES) with local refinement using gradient-based optimization. This dual-level strategy addresses the high-dimensional optimization challenge (n=180-360) and non-convex objective function. The CMA-ES provides broader exploration capability to avoid local optima, while local optimization ensures fine-grained convergence. \n\nKey limitations addressed:\n- Limited effectiveness of gradient-based methods in escaping local optima\n- Insufficient exploration by traditional local search alone\n- Inefficient evaluation of high-dimensional functions\n- Difficulty in handling both continuous and sum constraints simultaneously\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- n_points \u2208 {180, 360} (fixed interval [0,2] with dx=2/n_points)\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^{n_points}\n- target_sum = n_points/2 (fixed integral constraint)\n- Based on covariance matrix adaptation evolution strategy (CMA-ES) for global optimization\n- Local optimization uses gradient-based projected methods\n- Objective: min-max integral overlap C5 defined by finite convolution \n\nOperations:\n- Feasibility repair: project_to_sum(h, n_points/2) ensuring sum= n_points/2 and 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n- Objective computation: C5 = max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * (2/n_points)\n- Constraints: sum(h) = n_points/2 and 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\nImplementation notes:\n- Global optimization uses CMA-ES with adaptive covariance matrix\n- Local optimization uses projected gradient descent with precise derivatives\n- Dimension transfers: use interpolation/rigid scaling for alternative n_points\n- Repair mechanism: clip values to [0,1] then adjust sum using weighted averaging\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialization\n      i. Start with parent solution (180 points) if available\n      ii. Generate random feasible individuals using project_to_sum\n      iii. Create population for CMA-ES\n   \n   b. Global optimization (CMA-ES)\n      i. Initialize mean (initial population), step-size \u03c3, covariance matrix C\n      ii. While time_left > 0:\n          1. Generate d candidates from multivariate normal(\u03b8, \u03c3^2C)\n          2. Evaluate fitness for each candidate using repair\n          3. Update mean \u03b8 and covariance matrix C based on candidates\n          4. Update step-size \u03c3 using rank-1 covariance update\n          5. Select best candidate and update population\n   \n   c. Local optimization refinement\n      i. For best global candidate, perform projected gradient descent\n      ii. Compute exact gradient using:\n          \u2207objective = max_k (h(x) * (1-h(x+k))) * [k]   -> requires careful convolution differentiation\n      iii. Project gradients using Lagrange multipliers for both equality and inequality constraints\n      iv. Update until convergence or time limit\n\n   d. Search space management\n      i. Monitor time budget using time_left() assessment\n      ii. Limit evaluations based on dimensionality\n      iii. Maintain population diversity through periodic reinitialization\n\n4. Search choices\n   a. Initial population:\n      - Size: 40 for 180-dim, 30 for 360-dim\n      - Generation limit: 50 for 180-dim, 40 for 360-dim\n      - \u03bc/\u03bb CMA-ES ratio: 1/4 (standard mode)\n\n   b. CMA-ES parameters:\n      - Initial step size \u03c3: automatically adapted\n      - Constraint handling: repair mechanism only\n      - \u03bc_eff = (\u03bc+1)^2/(5\u03bc) for covariance update\n\n   c. Local optimization:\n      - Max iterations: 200\n      - Gradient tolerance: 1e-5\n      - Step size reduction if plateau detected\n\n   d. Dimension handling:\n      - Use tricubic interpolation for grid transfer between 180 and 360\n      - Preserve parent solution structure when transferring\n\n5. Compute budget\n   a. Global phase:\n      - Wall time estimation: O(n^3) per evaluation \n      - Per-generation cost: 500ms for 180-dim, 800ms for 360-dim\n      - Allowed generations: ~30-50 total across all dimensions\n\n   b. Local refinement:\n      - Cost reduces by factor of 2 per optimization step\n      - Reserve 25% budget for local refinement\n\n   c. Termination conditions:\n      - Time deadline reached (total budget 1300s)\n      - Repetition of best solution (n consecutive evaluations)\n      - Failure to improve (tolerance 1e-3)\n\n6. Validation and recovery\n   a. Feasibility checks:\n      - Ensure all evaluated h satisfy 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n      - Verify np.sum(h) == n_points/2\n      - Compute C5 using np.correlate(clip(h,0,1), mode='full')\n\n   b. Recovery fallback:\n      - If no valid solution after 2000s: fall back to initial_h_values\n      - If dimension conversion fails: revert to nearest neighbor solution\n      - Handle NaN/Inf values: default to uniform solution\n\n   c. Evaluation reversion:\n      - All C5 computations use finite precision tolerance 1e-12\n      - Validate final solution against test harness\n\n   d. Budget monitoring:\n      - Record wall-clock time per generation\n      - Early termination if total time approaching 1200s\n      - Dynamic generation scaling based on elapsed time\n\nThe algorithm prioritizes geometric transfer of solutions between resolutions first, then applies CMA-ES global refinement, concluding with local optimization. This balances exhaustive search with efficient convergence.",
